1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:04,298
Imagine a church with a roof, walls, windows and doors.

2
00:00:04,298 --> 00:00:06,532
Now imagine it was never built.

3
00:00:06,532 --> 00:00:08,695
Nobody stacked a single stone.

4
00:00:08,695 --> 00:00:12,300
It was cut out of the mountain, from the top down.

5
00:00:12,766 --> 00:00:15,368
Local tradition says angels helped.

6
00:00:15,368 --> 00:00:16,780
By day, men carved.

7
00:00:16,780 --> 00:00:19,605
By night, angels worked twice as fast.

8
00:00:19,605 --> 00:00:21,166
So who really did it?

9
00:00:21,633 --> 00:00:23,751
Tonight we open a survey report.

10
00:00:23,751 --> 00:00:30,966
Eleven churches, one mountain, and one question: how do you build a cathedral by removing the rock around it?

11
00:00:31,433 --> 00:00:37,056
We will check the legend, the dates, the method, and the alien version of the story.

12
00:00:37,056 --> 00:00:38,730
Every claim gets a score.

13
00:00:38,730 --> 00:00:39,533
Let's begin.

14
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:41,201
Eleven churches.

15
00:00:41,201 --> 00:00:42,928
One verdict at the end.

16
00:00:42,928 --> 00:00:48,033
Is Lalibela a miracle, a mystery, or something even more impressive?

17
00:00:52,500 --> 00:00:53,773
Survey sheet one.

18
00:00:53,773 --> 00:00:54,447
The site.

19
00:00:54,447 --> 00:01:03,733
Lalibela is a small town in northern Ethiopia, high in the mountains, about two thousand six hundred metres above sea level.

20
00:01:04,200 --> 00:01:12,129
It sits at the foot of Mount Abuna Yosef, roughly six hundred and forty kilometres from the capital, Addis Ababa.

21
00:01:12,129 --> 00:01:13,533
Far from everything.

22
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:18,415
A small river runs through it, named the Yordanos, after the Jordan.

23
00:01:18,415 --> 00:01:21,466
That name matters, and we will come back to it.

24
00:01:21,933 --> 00:01:23,795
Eleven churches stand here.

25
00:01:23,795 --> 00:01:25,451
Six in a northern group.

26
00:01:25,451 --> 00:01:27,176
Four in a southern group.

27
00:01:27,176 --> 00:01:29,866
And one alone, set apart from the rest.

28
00:01:30,333 --> 00:01:35,552
The groups are joined by tunnels, trenches and passages, all cut in the rock.

29
00:01:35,552 --> 00:01:40,500
Walking between them feels like walking through the inside of a mountain.

30
00:01:40,966 --> 00:01:45,932
The stone itself is volcanic: red scoria on top of darker basalt.

31
00:01:45,932 --> 00:01:48,759
Soft enough to carve with iron tools.

32
00:01:48,759 --> 00:01:51,433
Hard enough to stand for centuries.

33
00:01:51,900 --> 00:01:52,998
So far, simple.

34
00:01:52,998 --> 00:01:57,172
A mountain town, a river, eleven churches cut from stone.

35
00:01:57,172 --> 00:02:00,833
Now, the story people tell about who ordered them.

36
00:02:01,299 --> 00:02:02,584
Survey sheet two.

37
00:02:02,584 --> 00:02:03,264
The king.

38
00:02:03,264 --> 00:02:14,066
Tradition names him Gebre Mesqel Lalibela, a ruler of the Zagwe dynasty who reigned around the year eleven eighty one, until twelve twenty one.

39
00:02:14,533 --> 00:02:17,415
The story comes down in church tradition.

40
00:02:17,415 --> 00:02:24,866
As a child, a swarm of bees was said to circle him, and his mother saw it as a sign that he would be king.

41
00:02:25,333 --> 00:02:26,671
Then came a crisis.

42
00:02:26,671 --> 00:02:35,966
In that age, Muslim conquests had cut off the route to Jerusalem, and Christian pilgrims could no longer safely reach the holy city.

43
00:02:36,433 --> 00:02:41,449
The tradition says the king answered by building a new Jerusalem at home.

44
00:02:41,449 --> 00:02:46,466
Hence the river called Jordan, and hills and places given biblical names.

45
00:02:46,933 --> 00:02:55,266
One story says he was taken in a vision to see the real Jerusalem, and told to build its copy, in stone, for his people.

46
00:02:55,733 --> 00:02:58,976
Whatever the truth, the site still works as that.

47
00:02:58,976 --> 00:03:04,866
More than a hundred thousand pilgrims come every year, especially at Ethiopian Christmas.

48
00:03:05,333 --> 00:03:05,864
Logged.

49
00:03:05,864 --> 00:03:08,366
A king, a crisis, a holy purpose.

50
00:03:08,366 --> 00:03:10,414
But a legend is not a date.

51
00:03:10,414 --> 00:03:13,599
Next, how the churches were actually made.

52
00:03:14,066 --> 00:03:15,405
Survey sheet three.

53
00:03:15,405 --> 00:03:16,180
The method.

54
00:03:16,180 --> 00:03:19,351
This is the part that breaks people's brains.

55
00:03:19,351 --> 00:03:20,972
Normal buildings go up.

56
00:03:20,972 --> 00:03:22,099
These went down.

57
00:03:22,566 --> 00:03:31,033
First, workers cut a deep trench around a block of living rock, isolating it, like a giant island of stone in the mountain.

58
00:03:31,500 --> 00:03:38,699
Then they shaped the outside: the roof, the walls, the cornice, the carved window frames, all from the top downward.

59
00:03:39,166 --> 00:03:48,033
Then, and only then, they tunnelled inward, hollowing the inside, leaving columns and arches standing exactly where they were needed.

60
00:03:48,500 --> 00:03:50,465
This method has a harsh rule.

61
00:03:50,465 --> 00:03:52,227
You cannot make a mistake.

62
00:03:52,227 --> 00:03:54,735
Cut too much, and the rock stays cut.

63
00:03:54,735 --> 00:03:56,633
There is no putting it back.

64
00:03:57,099 --> 00:04:01,574
The tools were simple: iron picks, chisels, hammers, and wedges.

65
00:04:01,574 --> 00:04:02,903
Nothing mysterious.

66
00:04:02,903 --> 00:04:05,699
What was extraordinary was the planning.

67
00:04:06,166 --> 00:04:07,099
And the waste?

68
00:04:07,099 --> 00:04:11,699
Many thousands of tonnes of rock had to be carried out of those pits.

69
00:04:11,699 --> 00:04:15,099
Spoil heaps and terraces nearby show where it went.

70
00:04:15,566 --> 00:04:18,659
Look closely and the stone imitates timber.

71
00:04:18,659 --> 00:04:25,133
Carved beams, window frames and door posts copy the style of the great Aksumite buildings.

72
00:04:25,600 --> 00:04:31,363
The carvers copied architecture they knew, but in a medium that cannot be dismantled.

73
00:04:31,363 --> 00:04:33,533
It is architecture as sculpture.

74
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:34,990
Method scored.

75
00:04:34,990 --> 00:04:37,042
Real, human, and astonishing.

76
00:04:37,042 --> 00:04:40,933
But the most famous church deserves a sheet of its own.

77
00:04:41,399 --> 00:04:42,663
Survey sheet four.

78
00:04:42,663 --> 00:04:45,542
Bet Giyorgis, the Church of Saint George.

79
00:04:45,542 --> 00:04:49,333
The one set apart, and the one every photograph shows.

80
00:04:49,800 --> 00:04:58,133
Its roof is a cross, and the church itself is a cross, cut from a single block, about fifteen metres deep inside a square pit.

81
00:04:58,600 --> 00:05:02,702
You reach it by a narrow trench that slopes down into the pit.

82
00:05:02,702 --> 00:05:07,533
For a long moment you see nothing, then the roof appears below your feet.

83
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:08,852
Three doors.

84
00:05:08,852 --> 00:05:09,917
Twelve windows.

85
00:05:09,917 --> 00:05:11,834
Crosses carved on the roof.

86
00:05:11,834 --> 00:05:16,166
Every proportion calculated, and cut without a second chance.

87
00:05:16,633 --> 00:05:20,857
The courtyard around it is about twenty two metres square.

88
00:05:20,857 --> 00:05:23,115
Smooth walls, straight corners.

89
00:05:23,115 --> 00:05:25,299
Cut by hand, in volcanic rock.

90
00:05:25,766 --> 00:05:31,882
Tradition says Saint George rode his white horse here and insisted that his church be finished.

91
00:05:31,882 --> 00:05:34,199
It is the last church of the legend.

92
00:05:34,666 --> 00:05:37,071
But the pit has a weakness: water.

93
00:05:37,071 --> 00:05:39,404
Rain collects, seeps, and erodes.

94
00:05:39,404 --> 00:05:42,233
Conservators have fought it for decades.

95
00:05:42,699 --> 00:05:43,192
Scored.

96
00:05:43,192 --> 00:05:45,727
A masterpiece, and a very human one.

97
00:05:45,727 --> 00:05:48,966
Now the question that splits historians: when?

98
00:05:49,433 --> 00:05:50,707
Survey sheet five.

99
00:05:50,707 --> 00:05:52,052
The dating problem.

100
00:05:52,052 --> 00:05:56,725
The tradition says all eleven were made in just twenty four years.

101
00:05:56,725 --> 00:05:58,566
The ground says otherwise.

102
00:05:59,033 --> 00:06:03,518
In two thousand nine, a French team mapped the whole site in detail.

103
00:06:03,518 --> 00:06:08,399
They studied how the trenches cut into each other, like layers in a stack.

104
00:06:08,866 --> 00:06:12,561
If one trench cuts through another, the cut one is older.

105
00:06:12,561 --> 00:06:17,100
That simple rule can order the work, without any carbon dating at all.

106
00:06:17,566 --> 00:06:22,521
The result: several phases of work, four or five, spread across centuries.

107
00:06:22,521 --> 00:06:25,399
Some parts may be much older than the king.

108
00:06:25,866 --> 00:06:35,233
Scholars suggest early elements might date from the seventh century or even before, with the main burst of work around the twelfth and thirteenth.

109
00:06:35,699 --> 00:06:38,729
Honestly, the exact dates are not settled.

110
00:06:38,729 --> 00:06:41,397
No inscription says here is the year.

111
00:06:41,397 --> 00:06:43,633
The scholars say: undetermined.

112
00:06:44,100 --> 00:06:47,183
No contemporary account describes the building.

113
00:06:47,183 --> 00:06:52,433
The earliest stories were written down later, and written to praise a holy king.

114
00:06:52,899 --> 00:06:56,923
So the king may have started, or expanded, or inherited.

115
00:06:56,923 --> 00:07:02,600
A legend can be a compressed memory of centuries of work, credited to one name.

116
00:07:03,066 --> 00:07:04,097
Dating scored.

117
00:07:04,097 --> 00:07:06,896
The twenty four year tale is unlikely.

118
00:07:06,896 --> 00:07:10,800
Phases across centuries is the best evidence we have.

119
00:07:11,266 --> 00:07:12,442
Survey sheet six.

120
00:07:12,442 --> 00:07:13,341
The theories.

121
00:07:13,341 --> 00:07:17,975
Online, you will hear that human beings could never have done this.

122
00:07:17,975 --> 00:07:22,333
That it needed lost technology, or visitors from another world.

123
00:07:22,800 --> 00:07:25,143
Claim one: too perfect, too big.

124
00:07:25,143 --> 00:07:29,464
But the church is large for a rock carving, not impossible.

125
00:07:29,464 --> 00:07:32,833
Rock cut architecture exists across the world.

126
00:07:33,300 --> 00:07:37,958
Petra in Jordan, the Ajanta caves in India, Cappadocia in Turkey.

127
00:07:37,958 --> 00:07:43,333
People have been carving buildings from living rock for thousands of years.

128
00:07:43,800 --> 00:07:46,820
And here, the chisel marks are still visible.

129
00:07:46,820 --> 00:07:50,042
Tool strokes, corrections, and unfinished areas.

130
00:07:50,042 --> 00:07:53,600
That is the fingerprint of human work, not a machine.

131
00:07:54,066 --> 00:07:56,432
Claim two: no tools could cut it.

132
00:07:56,432 --> 00:08:03,600
But iron tools were certainly available in medieval Ethiopia, and the rock is soft when freshly cut.

133
00:08:04,066 --> 00:08:06,733
Claim three: no workforce could do it.

134
00:08:06,733 --> 00:08:14,033
Yet a community that large, over generations, with priests, masons and labourers, is entirely plausible.

135
00:08:14,500 --> 00:08:17,485
As for angels, that is faith, not evidence.

136
00:08:17,485 --> 00:08:22,899
It is a beautiful way for a culture to say: this was too good to be only ours.

137
00:08:23,366 --> 00:08:27,928
There is no trace of any alien object, tool, or technology here.

138
00:08:27,928 --> 00:08:32,633
The only mystery is how a human community pulled off the planning.

139
00:08:33,100 --> 00:08:34,278
Theories scored.

140
00:08:34,278 --> 00:08:40,173
Evidence is strong for human hands, and the alien claim has nothing to stand on.

141
00:08:40,173 --> 00:08:41,500
One sheet remains.

142
00:08:41,966 --> 00:08:43,242
Survey sheet seven.

143
00:08:43,242 --> 00:08:44,450
The living church.

144
00:08:44,450 --> 00:08:45,726
This is not a ruin.

145
00:08:45,726 --> 00:08:51,566
For more than eight hundred years, the churches have been used, almost without a break.

146
00:08:52,033 --> 00:08:53,861
That is also their problem.

147
00:08:53,861 --> 00:09:00,700
Rain and heavy visits are wearing the soft stone, and huge shelters now protect some of the churches.

148
00:09:01,166 --> 00:09:07,821
UNESCO listed Lalibela in nineteen seventy eight, among the very first World Heritage sites.

149
00:09:07,821 --> 00:09:11,366
It is a place of worship first, a mystery second.

150
00:09:11,833 --> 00:09:12,452
Debrief.

151
00:09:12,452 --> 00:09:14,233
Time to score Lalibela.

152
00:09:14,700 --> 00:09:19,328
Eleven churches carved from living rock, top down: evidence.

153
00:09:19,328 --> 00:09:22,799
Cut with iron tools by human hands: evidence.

154
00:09:22,799 --> 00:09:26,193
A working holy site for centuries: evidence.

155
00:09:26,193 --> 00:09:29,433
The king as organiser: plausible, undated.

156
00:09:29,899 --> 00:09:32,962
All of it in twenty four years: unlikely.

157
00:09:32,962 --> 00:09:38,565
Built by angels or aliens: no evidence, and the chisel marks say otherwise.

158
00:09:38,565 --> 00:09:42,600
The exact date of each church: still an open question.

159
00:09:43,066 --> 00:09:43,916
The verdict.

160
00:09:43,916 --> 00:09:45,049
Real, and human.

161
00:09:45,049 --> 00:09:47,174
Lalibela is not an alien site.

162
00:09:47,174 --> 00:09:55,533
It is something harder to believe: a community that planned a masterpiece they could never undo, and cut it perfectly.

163
00:09:56,000 --> 00:10:03,906
Somewhere, centuries ago, a mason made the first cut at the top of a block of mountain, knowing it could never be undone.

164
00:10:03,906 --> 00:10:05,866
That is faith, and it is real.

165
00:10:06,333 --> 00:10:13,079
Next case: a freight plane, a night sky off the coast of New Zealand, and a light that followed the crew.

166
00:10:13,079 --> 00:10:14,299
What did they film?

167
00:10:14,766 --> 00:10:19,180
What do you think is the greater miracle: the angels, or the masons?

168
00:10:19,180 --> 00:10:24,633
Tell us in the comments, and subscribe for the next ancient mystery put to the test.
